//! image/cluster_adjacency.rye -- the CLUSTER GRAPH above the object graph: how two whole connected CLUSTERS sit relative to each other --
//! the true inter-cluster DISTANCE (the min nearest-pixel gap over every member pair), whether their union FRAMES overlap, and each
//! cluster's NEAREST other cluster. Read straight from image/overlap_clusters.rye's labeling, image/cluster_facts.rye's union frames, and
//! image/scene_graph.rye's precomputed pair matrix -- exact integers, NO float, NO tolerance.
//!
//! Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- image/object_relation.rye reads how a PAIR of objects sit relative to each other; image/scene_graph.rye
//! joins that pair relation over EVERY pair into one matrix and reads each object's nearest NEIGHBOR; image/overlap_clusters.rye gathers the
//! objects whose boxes overlap into connected CLUSTERS; image/cluster_facts.rye reads each cluster's union FRAME and joined facts. Those name
//! WHO belongs and WHAT each cluster is. This rung lifts the pair read one level higher, to the clusters themselves: it reads how two whole
//! clusters sit relative to each other, so a surface can ask which connected regions of a layout stand nearest, which frames overlap, and
//! which region a given region is drawn toward.
//!
//! The DECISIVE crux -- the box-is-a-prefilter lesson lifted to the CLUSTER PAIR, in two shapes at once:
//!
//!   TWO DISTINCT CLUSTERS SHARE NO EDGE YET THEIR FRAMES CAN OVERLAP. Distinct clusters can never share a box-overlap edge between their
//!   members -- that is exactly why they are distinct clusters, since connectivity is the transitive closure of box overlap. Yet their union
//!   FRAMES can still overlap: image/cluster_facts.rye proves one cluster's frame can wholly contain an object of a different cluster. So
//!   `cluster_frames_overlap` reads TRUE for two clusters that share no member edge at all -- the frame a surface draws around a connected
//!   region over-claims contact with a neighbor the clusters never touch. Frame overlap is a hint, never the adjacency test.
//!
//!   THE TRUE CLUSTER DISTANCE IS A REAL GRADED GAP, FLOORED AT 2. `cluster_distance` reads the minimum exact nearest-pixel Chebyshev
//!   distance over every member pair (one object from each cluster), so a box comparison BOUNDS the clusters' true nearness yet never
//!   DETERMINES it: two clusters whose frames overlap can sit 2 pixels apart, a nearer pair drags the distance down while a far one is
//!   ignored, and the distance grades up for a truly far cluster. It is floored at 2 because two distinct clusters hold distinct
//!   8-components, never 8-adjacent. And `nearest_cluster` is DIRECTED, exactly as image/scene_graph.rye's nearest_neighbor is: a cluster
//!   can be another's nearest without that other being its own.
//!
//! Compose not braid. This rung OWNS only the cluster-pair reads -- the min over member pairs, the frame overlap test, and the directed
//! nearest. No cluster recomputed (image/overlap_clusters.rye owns the connectivity), no frame recomputed (image/cluster_facts.rye owns each
//! cluster's union box), no distance recomputed (image/scene_graph.rye owns each pair's exact nearest-pixel gap, read here through its matrix).
//! A reading over a reading over a reading over a reading.
//!
//! Everything is bounded before a byte: the scene graph caps its object count at `scene_graph_max_objects`, so every member-pair scan runs a
//! bounded number of pairs and every nearest walk over the clusters is bounded by the cluster count. A cluster id of zero or past the cluster
//! count refuses `BadClusterId`; a distance or frame-overlap read of a cluster against itself refuses `SameCluster`, having named nothing; a
//! nearest read of a scene with only one cluster refuses `NoOtherCluster`. No network, no key, no funds -- the cluster-to-cluster reads a
//! surface names the day it must point one connected region toward its nearest neighbor, on our own geometry.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run image/cluster_adjacency.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const label = @import("label.rye");
const qoi = @import("qoi.rye");
const scene_manifest = @import("scene_manifest.rye");
const scene_graph = @import("scene_graph.rye");
const overlap_clusters = @import("overlap_clusters.rye");
const cluster_facts = @import("cluster_facts.rye");

pub const cluster_adjacency_version = "20260819.011558";

pub const ClusterAdjacencyError = error{
    BadClusterId,
    SameCluster,
    NoOtherCluster,
};

/// Guard a cluster id -- a real dense cluster label (1..clusters). Refuses `BadClusterId` at the edge before any member is read.
fn validate_cid(clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, cid: u32) ClusterAdjacencyError!void {
    // invariant: one cluster id per object.
    assert(clustering.cluster_id.len == @as(usize, clustering.components));
    // invariant: a real clustering holds at least one cluster.
    assert(clustering.clusters >= 1);
    if (cid == 0 or cid > clustering.clusters) return error.BadClusterId;
}

/// The true DISTANCE between clusters `cid_a` and `cid_b` -- the minimum exact nearest-pixel Chebyshev gap over every member pair, one object
/// drawn from each cluster. A nearer pair drags the distance down; a far pair is ignored. Floored structurally at 2, since two members of
/// distinct clusters are distinct 8-components and never 8-adjacent. Refuses `BadClusterId` for a bad id, `SameCluster` for a cluster
/// weighed against itself (a distance of a cluster to itself names nothing).
pub fn cluster_distance(clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, cid_a: u32, cid_b: u32) !u32 {
    // invariant: the clustering and the graph describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == graph.components);
    // invariant: the object count stays within the scene graph's named ceiling, so the member-pair scan is bounded.
    assert(graph.components <= scene_graph.scene_graph_max_objects);
    try validate_cid(clustering, cid_a);
    try validate_cid(clustering, cid_b);
    if (cid_a == cid_b) return error.SameCluster;

    var found: bool = false;
    var best: u32 = 0;
    var a: u32 = 1;
    while (a <= clustering.components) : (a += 1) {
        if (clustering.cluster_id[a - 1] != cid_a) continue;
        var b: u32 = 1;
        while (b <= clustering.components) : (b += 1) {
            if (clustering.cluster_id[b - 1] != cid_b) continue;
            // a and b belong to distinct clusters, so they are distinct objects -- never a self relation.
            const rel = try scene_graph.relation_of(graph, a, b);
            if (!found or rel.nearest_cheby < best) {
                best = rel.nearest_cheby;
                found = true;
            }
        }
    }

    // invariant: two real clusters each hold at least one member, so at least one member pair was weighed.
    assert(found);
    // invariant: distinct clusters hold distinct 8-components, never 8-adjacent, so the true gap is at least two.
    assert(best >= 2);
    return best;
}

/// TRUE when the union FRAMES of clusters `cid_a` and `cid_b` overlap -- a box comparison over image/cluster_facts.rye's cluster frames. This
/// is the read that makes the over-claim plain: it can be TRUE for two clusters whose members share no edge and stand 2 or more pixels apart,
/// since a cluster's frame can enclose the frame of a different cluster. Overlap of frames, never adjacency of members. Refuses
/// `BadClusterId` for a bad id, `SameCluster` for a cluster weighed against itself.
pub fn cluster_frames_overlap(clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, cid_a: u32, cid_b: u32) !bool {
    // invariant: the clustering and the graph describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == graph.components);
    try validate_cid(clustering, cid_a);
    try validate_cid(clustering, cid_b);
    if (cid_a == cid_b) return error.SameCluster;

    const fa = try cluster_facts.cluster_bbox(clustering, graph, cid_a);
    const fb = try cluster_facts.cluster_bbox(clustering, graph, cid_b);
    // invariant: both frames are well-formed on both axes before overlap is judged.
    assert(fa.max_x >= fa.min_x);
    assert(fa.max_y >= fa.min_y);
    assert(fb.max_x >= fb.min_x);
    assert(fb.max_y >= fb.min_y);
    // two inclusive boxes overlap exactly when they meet on both axes.
    const on_x = fa.min_x <= fb.max_x and fb.min_x <= fa.max_x;
    const on_y = fa.min_y <= fb.max_y and fb.min_y <= fa.max_y;
    return on_x and on_y;
}

/// The ID of the cluster NEAREST to `cid` -- the other cluster of least true `cluster_distance`, ties broken by the earliest cluster id so
/// the read is deterministic. DIRECTED, exactly as image/scene_graph.rye's nearest_neighbor: a cluster can be another's nearest without that
/// other being its own. Refuses `BadClusterId` for a bad id, `NoOtherCluster` when the scene holds only one cluster (nothing to be near).
pub fn nearest_cluster(clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, cid: u32) !u32 {
    // invariant: the clustering and the graph describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == graph.components);
    try validate_cid(clustering, cid);
    if (clustering.clusters < 2) return error.NoOtherCluster;

    var found: bool = false;
    var best_id: u32 = 0;
    var best_dist: u32 = 0;
    var other: u32 = 1;
    while (other <= clustering.clusters) : (other += 1) {
        if (other == cid) continue;
        const dist = try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cid, other);
        // strictly less keeps the earliest id on a distance tie -- deterministic.
        if (!found or dist < best_dist) {
            best_dist = dist;
            best_id = other;
            found = true;
        }
    }

    // invariant: a scene of two or more clusters always names a real nearest other cluster.
    assert(found);
    assert(best_id >= 1);
    assert(best_id <= clustering.clusters);
    // invariant: a cluster is never its own nearest.
    assert(best_id != cid);
    return best_id;
}

// --- selftest: the DECISIVE box-is-a-prefilter crux lifted to the CLUSTER PAIR -- a three-cluster scene on a 15x15 canvas. Cluster 1 is two
//     objects reaching apart (a corner-L A and a ring B) whose boxes overlap through a shared corner, connected; cluster 2 is a lone block C
//     sitting in the top-right gap, its box inside cluster 1's union frame yet its own singleton; cluster 3 is a far lone block D at the
//     bottom corner. cluster_frames_overlap reads TRUE for clusters 1 and 2 (C's frame lies inside cluster 1's frame) though they share no
//     member edge and stand 2 pixels apart (frames overlap, clusters distinct); cluster_distance grades 2, 3, 10 over the three cluster
//     pairs (a real graded gap, floored at 2); nearest_cluster is DIRECTED -- cluster 3's nearest is cluster 1, yet cluster 1's nearest is
//     cluster 2, not cluster 3; refusals by name; determinism ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Mint a one-color RGBA canvas -- a local, bounded builder so this rung leans on no higher module.
fn make_canvas(garden: std.mem.Allocator, w: u32, h: u32, fill: u8) !qoi.Pixmap {
    // invariant: a canvas has real extent on both axes.
    assert(w >= 1);
    assert(h >= 1);
    const total: u64 = @as(u64, w) * @as(u64, h);
    // invariant: the canvas stays within the image module's named pixel ceiling.
    assert(total <= qoi.qoi_max_pixels);
    const pixels = try garden.alloc(u8, @as(usize, @intCast(total)) * 4);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < pixels.len) : (i += 4) {
        pixels[i] = fill;
        pixels[i + 1] = fill;
        pixels[i + 2] = fill;
        pixels[i + 3] = 255;
    }
    return qoi.Pixmap{ .width = w, .height = h, .channels = 4, .colorspace = 0, .pixels = pixels };
}

/// Set one foreground pixel at (x, y).
fn set_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Paint a corner bracket-L: a vertical arm down column `x` from `y0` to `y1`, and a horizontal arm along row `yr` from `x0` to `x1`.
fn set_corner_l(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y0: u32, y1: u32, yr: u32, x0: u32, x1: u32) void {
    // invariant: the arms run in the positive direction and stay inside the buffer.
    assert(y1 >= y0);
    assert(x1 >= x0);
    assert(x1 < img.width);
    assert(y1 < img.height);
    var yy: u32 = y0;
    while (yy <= y1) : (yy += 1) set_dot(img, x, yy);
    var xx: u32 = x0;
    while (xx <= x1) : (xx += 1) set_dot(img, xx, yr);
}

/// Paint a 1-pixel-thick RING (a rectangle outline) with corners `(x0, y0)` and `(x1, y1)` -- a holed object, its interior background.
fn set_ring(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x0: u32, y0: u32, x1: u32, y1: u32) void {
    // invariant: the ring spans at least a 3x3 box so it encloses a real interior hole, and stays inside the buffer.
    assert(x1 >= x0 + 2);
    assert(y1 >= y0 + 2);
    assert(x1 < img.width);
    assert(y1 < img.height);
    var xx: u32 = x0;
    while (xx <= x1) : (xx += 1) {
        set_dot(img, xx, y0);
        set_dot(img, xx, y1);
    }
    var yy: u32 = y0;
    while (yy <= y1) : (yy += 1) {
        set_dot(img, x0, yy);
        set_dot(img, x1, yy);
    }
}

/// Paint a solid `bw` x `bh` foreground block with its top-left corner at (x, y).
fn set_block(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32, bw: u32, bh: u32) void {
    // invariant: the block stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x + bw <= img.width);
    assert(y + bh <= img.height);
    var yy: u32 = y;
    while (yy < y + bh) : (yy += 1) {
        var xx: u32 = x;
        while (xx < x + bw) : (xx += 1) set_dot(img, xx, yy);
    }
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    // --- BUILD THE THREE-CLUSTER SCENE on a 15x15 canvas:
    //     object A (label 1): a corner-L opening lower-right -- vertical arm col x=1 rows 1..6, horizontal arm row y=1 cols 1..6, box
    //         (1,1)-(6,6), area 11;
    //     object C (label 2): a 2x2 block at (8,1), box (8,1)-(9,2), area 4, in the top-right gap;
    //     object B (label 3): a 6x6 RING with corners (4,4) and (9,9), box (4,4)-(9,9), area 20, holed;
    //     object D (label 4): a 2x2 block at (12,12), box (12,12)-(13,13), area 4, far in the bottom-right corner.
    //     A's box and B's box overlap at the shared corner (4,4)-(6,6), so A and B connect into cluster 1. C's box overlaps neither, its
    //     own cluster 2. D's box overlaps neither, its own cluster 3. Raster labeling numbers A=1 ((1,1)), C=2 ((8,1)), B=3 ((4,4)),
    //     D=4 ((12,12)) ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var scene = try make_canvas(garden, 15, 15, 0);
    set_corner_l(&scene, 1, 1, 6, 1, 1, 6); // A: col1 rows1..6, row1 cols1..6
    set_block(&scene, 8, 1, 2, 2); // C: block at (8,1), box (8,1)-(9,2)
    set_ring(&scene, 4, 4, 9, 9); // B: ring box (4,4)-(9,9)
    set_block(&scene, 12, 12, 2, 2); // D: far block at (12,12), box (12,12)-(13,13)

    const map = try label.label_components(garden, &scene);
    if (map.count != 4) return error.SceneNotFourComponents;
    const manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &map);
    const graph = try scene_graph.build_scene_graph(garden, manifest, &map);
    if (scene_graph.object_count(graph) != 4) return error.GraphNotFourObjects;

    const clustering = try overlap_clusters.overlap_clusters(garden, graph);
    if (overlap_clusters.cluster_count(clustering) != 3) return error.ClusterCountNotThree;
    const cl_a = try overlap_clusters.cluster_of(clustering, 1); // cluster 1: {A, B}
    const cl_c = try overlap_clusters.cluster_of(clustering, 2); // cluster 2: {C}
    const cl_b = try overlap_clusters.cluster_of(clustering, 3); // B shares cluster 1
    const cl_d = try overlap_clusters.cluster_of(clustering, 4); // cluster 3: {D}
    if (cl_a != cl_b) return error.ABNotOneCluster;
    if (cl_a == cl_c or cl_a == cl_d or cl_c == cl_d) return error.ClustersNotDistinct;

    // --- TWO DISTINCT CLUSTERS SHARE NO EDGE YET THEIR FRAMES CAN OVERLAP -- cluster 1's union frame spans (1,1)-(9,9) and holds C's frame
    //     (8,1)-(9,2) wholly inside, so cluster_frames_overlap(1, 2) is TRUE, though cluster 1 and cluster 2 share no member edge (they are
    //     distinct clusters exactly because no box overlaps between them) and stand apart -----------------------------------------------
    if (try cluster_frames_overlap(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_c) != true) return error.FramesOneTwoNotOverlapping;
    // yet the two clusters share no member edge: no member of cluster 1 has a box overlapping any member of cluster 2 (else they would be
    // one cluster) -- overlap_clusters proves them distinct, so frame overlap over-claims contact the members never make
    if (try overlap_clusters.same_cluster(clustering, 1, 2) != false) return error.ACFalselyClustered;
    if (try overlap_clusters.same_cluster(clustering, 3, 2) != false) return error.BCFalselyClustered;
    // the far cluster 3's frame overlaps neither -- a true non-overlap, unlike the over-claiming pair
    if (try cluster_frames_overlap(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_d) != false) return error.FramesOneThreeFalselyOverlap;
    if (try cluster_frames_overlap(clustering, graph, cl_c, cl_d) != false) return error.FramesTwoThreeFalselyOverlap;

    // --- THE TRUE CLUSTER DISTANCE IS A REAL GRADED GAP, FLOORED AT 2 -- the min nearest-pixel gap over member pairs grades 2, 3, 10 over
    //     the three cluster pairs, so a frame overlap never determines the true distance: clusters 1 and 2 overlap in frame yet sit 2 apart
    const d12 = try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_c);
    const d13 = try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_d);
    const d23 = try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_c, cl_d);
    if (d12 != 2) return error.DistOneTwoNotTwo; // frames overlap, yet the clusters stand 2 pixels apart
    if (d13 != 3) return error.DistOneThreeNotThree;
    if (d23 != 10) return error.DistTwoThreeNotTen;
    // the distance is symmetric -- the min over member pairs does not depend on the order of the two clusters
    if (try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_c, cl_a) != d12) return error.DistanceNotSymmetric;
    // floored at 2: no cluster pair reads below the distinct-8-component floor
    if (d12 < 2 or d13 < 2 or d23 < 2) return error.DistanceBelowFloor;

    // --- NEAREST_CLUSTER IS DIRECTED -- cluster 3 (the far block D) has cluster 1 as its nearest (gap 3, nearer than cluster 2's gap 10),
    //     yet cluster 1's nearest is cluster 2 (gap 2, nearer than cluster 3's gap 3), NOT cluster 3: a cluster can be another's nearest
    //     without that other being its own, exactly as scene_graph's nearest_neighbor is directed -----------------------------------------
    if (try nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, cl_d) != cl_a) return error.FarNearestNotClusterOne;
    if (try nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, cl_a) != cl_c) return error.OneNearestNotClusterTwo;
    if (try nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, cl_a) == cl_d) return error.OneNearestFalselyClusterThree;
    if (try nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, cl_c) != cl_a) return error.TwoNearestNotClusterOne;

    // --- REFUSALS -- a zero or past-the-count cluster id refuses BadClusterId; a cluster weighed against itself refuses SameCluster --------
    if (cluster_distance(clustering, graph, 0, 1)) |_| return error.ZeroCidDistNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadClusterId) return error.WrongZeroDistError;
    }
    if (cluster_distance(clustering, graph, 1, 4)) |_| return error.PastCidDistNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadClusterId) return error.WrongPastDistError;
    }
    if (cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_a)) |_| return error.SameCidDistNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.SameCluster) return error.WrongSameDistError;
    }
    if (cluster_frames_overlap(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_a)) |_| return error.SameCidFramesNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.SameCluster) return error.WrongSameFramesError;
    }
    if (nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroCidNearestNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadClusterId) return error.WrongZeroNearestError;
    }

    // --- A LONE-CLUSTER SCENE -- one connected cluster has no other cluster to be near, so nearest_cluster refuses NoOtherCluster ---------
    var lone_img = try make_canvas(garden, 6, 6, 0);
    set_block(&lone_img, 1, 1, 2, 2); // one object, one cluster
    const lone_map = try label.label_components(garden, &lone_img);
    if (lone_map.count != 1) return error.LoneNotOneComponent;
    const lone_manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &lone_map);
    const lone_graph = try scene_graph.build_scene_graph(garden, lone_manifest, &lone_map);
    const lone_clustering = try overlap_clusters.overlap_clusters(garden, lone_graph);
    if (overlap_clusters.cluster_count(lone_clustering) != 1) return error.LoneClusterCountNotOne;
    if (nearest_cluster(lone_clustering, lone_graph, 1)) |_| return error.LoneNearestNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.NoOtherCluster) return error.WrongLoneNearestError;
    }

    // --- DETERMINISM -- the same clusters read the same distance and nearest twice -----------------------------------------------------
    if (try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_c) != try cluster_distance(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_c)) return error.DistanceNotDeterministic;
    if (try nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, cl_d) != try nearest_cluster(clustering, graph, cl_d)) return error.NearestNotDeterministic;
    if (try cluster_frames_overlap(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_c) != try cluster_frames_overlap(clustering, graph, cl_a, cl_c)) return error.FramesNotDeterministic;

    print("GREEN image-cluster-adjacency: the CLUSTER GRAPH above the object graph -- how two whole connected clusters sit relative to each other: the true inter-cluster distance (min nearest-pixel Chebyshev gap over every member pair), whether their union frames overlap, and each cluster's nearest other cluster, read straight from overlap_clusters' labeling, cluster_facts' union frames, and scene_graph's precomputed matrix, exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance -- the DECISIVE box-is-a-prefilter crux lifted to the CLUSTER PAIR, in two shapes. TWO DISTINCT CLUSTERS SHARE NO EDGE YET THEIR FRAMES CAN OVERLAP -- a corner-L A and a ring B reaching apart connect into cluster 1 (frame (1,1)-(9,9)), a lone block C is cluster 2 (frame (8,1)-(9,2)) sitting wholly inside cluster 1's frame, and a far block D is cluster 3 (frame (12,12)-(13,13)); cluster_frames_overlap(1,2) reads TRUE though clusters 1 and 2 share no member edge (they are distinct clusters exactly because no box overlaps between them) and stand apart, so frame overlap over-claims a contact the members never make, while the far cluster 3's frame overlaps neither. THE TRUE CLUSTER DISTANCE IS A REAL GRADED GAP, FLOORED AT 2 -- cluster_distance grades 2, 3, 10 over the three pairs, so a frame overlap never determines the true nearness: clusters 1 and 2 overlap in frame yet sit exactly 2 pixels apart, the floor for distinct 8-components; the distance is symmetric. And nearest_cluster is DIRECTED, exactly as scene_graph's nearest_neighbor -- cluster 3's nearest is cluster 1 (gap 3 under cluster 2's gap 10), yet cluster 1's nearest is cluster 2 (gap 2 under cluster 3's gap 3), NOT cluster 3, so a cluster can be another's nearest without that other being its own; a zero or past-the-count cluster id refuses BadClusterId, a cluster against itself refuses SameCluster, a lone-cluster scene refuses NoOtherCluster; the same clusters read the same distance and nearest twice. Compose not braid over image/overlap_clusters.rye (the connectivity and labeling), image/cluster_facts.rye (each cluster's union frame), image/scene_graph.rye (the precomputed pair matrix), and image/object_relation.rye (each pair's exact nearest-pixel distance)\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(garden);
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) {
        return run_selftest(garden);
    }
    print("usage: cluster_adjacency selftest\n", .{});
    return 1;
}
